      "text": "Let's start by analyzing the given equation: \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\).\n\nWe can use the identity for the product-to-sum identities, which states:\n\\[\n\\sin A \\sin B = \\frac{1}{2} (\\cos (A - B) - \\cos (A + B))\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\cos A \\cos B = \\frac{1}{2} (\\cos (A - B) + \\cos (A + B)).\n\\]\n\nApplying this to our equation:\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\frac{1}{2} (\\cos 2x \\cos 3x)\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\\[\n2 \\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\n\\]\n\nNext, we'll use the trigonometric identity for the product of sines:\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (2x - 3x) - \\cos (2x + 3x)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (-x) - \\cos (5x)]\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\cos (-x) = \\cos x\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos x - \\cos 5x]\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this into the equation:\n\\[\n2 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos x - \\cos 5x] \\right) = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos x - \\cos 5x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\n\\]\n\nWe know from trigonometric identities:\n\\[\n\\cos (A + B) = \\cos A \\cos B - \\sin A \\sin B\n\\]\n\nThus, substituting suitable values:\n\\[\n\\cos x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x + \\sin 2x \\sin 3x\n\\]\n\nNow we can try different angles that satisfies the original trigonometric equation and validate our results.\n\nWe will use Python to test for different values of \\(x\\) and find the smallest positive \\(x\\).\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport math\n\n# Define the equation and the range for testing\ndef equation(x):\n    return np.sin(2*x) * np.sin(3*x) - np.cos(2*x) * np.cos(3*x)\n\n# Test values of x from 0 to 360 degrees\ntest_values = np.linspace(0, 360, 361)\nvalid_x = None\n\nfor x in test_values:\n    if np.isclose(equation(x), 0, atol=1e-5):\n        valid_x = x\n        break\n\nprint(valid_x)\n```\n```output\n330.0\n```\nThe smallest positive angle \\(x\\) that satisfies \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\) is \\(\\boxed{330}\\) degrees.",
