      "text": "We start with the given equation \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\). Using the trigonometric identity for the cosine of a sum, \\(\\cos A \\cos B - \\sin A \\sin B = \\cos (A + B)\\), we can rewrite the right-hand side as:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 2x \\cos 3x - \\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos (2x + 3x) = \\cos 5x.\n\\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x - \\cos 2x \\cos 3x = \\cos 5x. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWe recognize that \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x - \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\) is equal to \\(-\\cos (2x + 3x)\\) by using the identity \\(\\sin A \\sin B - \\cos A \\cos B = -\\cos (A+B)\\). Therefore, equation (1) can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\n-\\cos 5x = \\cos 5x. \\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n-\\cos 5x = \\cos 5x,\n\\]\n\nwhich implies:\n\n\\[\n2 \\cos 5x = 0,\n\\]\n\nor\n\n\\[\n\\cos 5x = 0.\n\\]\n\nThe cosine function is zero at odd multiples of \\(90^\\circ\\), so we have:\n\n\\[\n5x = 90^\\circ + 180^\\circ k \\quad \\text{for integer } k.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx = 18^\\circ + 36^\\circ k \\quad \\text{for integer } k.\n\\]\n\nWe are looking for the smallest positive angle, so we choose \\(k = 0\\), giving us:\n\n\\[\nx = 18^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest positive angle \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{18}.\n\\]",
